## ===========================================================================
## THE GALLOWS AND THE RAID ON MAIDEN HOUSE — Book 9 (`story.ni:9581-9691`)
## and Book 10 (`story.ni:9692-10007`), Chapter 7.
##
## The change document ("Chapter 7 — The Gallows and the Raid on Maiden
## House", 2026-09-01/02) authorizes both books whole, from the butler's side
## door to the privy window: the crowd on East Commerce Street, the gallows
## in the square, Fossville's three beats and the blackout, the waking in her
## bunk with Fiona, the pounding on the door, the closet and the six rows
## heard through the wall, Theresa's expulsion, the key, the window. Every
## line is Gentry's; conversations are STUBS under the standing rule. The
## raid's layer on Maiden House's rooms and widows lives in
## `maiden-house.chord`, the hanging's layer on the two streets in
## `commerce-street.chord` and `lords-market.chord`; this file carries what is
## new — the square's people, the closet, and the two clocks the chapter
## runs on.
##
## THE RULINGS (change document, Chapter 7):
##   * EXTENT — the hanging and the raid together; Shannon climbing out after
##     her (`story.ni:10020`) is Chapter 8's first beat, so the window prints
##     nothing of its own here and Chapter VIII's row begins as she goes
##     through it (`the raid becomes escaped`).
##   * THE SQUARE — new content, fear without teeth: mercenaries ringing the
##     platform and in the crowd on the way, and she keeps her head down.
##     TWO placeholders: East Commerce Street's first entry with the crowd,
##     and the square's first entry before the execution fires (gap 1). The
##     set piece plays untouched.
##   * THE TRIGGER — the hanging begins when the butler shoves her out
##     (Chapter 6's fourth gap): the crowd and the gallows are placed by the
##     butler's release clause (`black-gate.chord`), before her first room
##     prints. (A `when the butler moves` clause here did not fire on his
##     move offstage — an offstage move raises no move event, GH #373.)
##   * WHAT THE MERCENARIES KNOW — they still hunt the boy Jack: the captain's
##     "she"/"the girl" through the wall convert to "he"/"the boy", his
##     capture-line "boy" carries. Theresa's raid words are the boy words
##     ("There he is!"; "he has done something unconscionable").
##   * SHANNON'S RUSE — she screams "Jacqueline" anyway; the captain does not
##     connect it. Carried verbatim.
##   * FIONA AND THE NAME — knowledge from the Duke; `FI28`/`FI30` carry as
##     written, "like a daughter" included (stubs name them). Her bedside and
##     raid "Jacqueline"s before that are the source's and carry with
##     `## DAVID:` marks, as Chapter 3's do.
##
## STUBS TAKING THE SOURCE'S PATH (flagged, per the standing rule's item 4):
## `talk to fiona` at the bedside prints the FI22-FI25 TODO and ENDS the
## scene — the raid begins on that turn, as FI25's firing begins it in the
## source. `talk to fiona` in the hallway after the expulsion prints the
## FI26-FI30 TODO and gives the brass key, as FI30 does.
##
## THE TWO CLOCKS: the execution is one turn after her first entry to the
## square (the designers' own note: waiting on `x gallows` "is stupid"), then
## one beat a turn — B_H1 on the expiry, B_H2 and B_H3 on two more one-turn
## clocks — and the blackout on the third. THE BANGING starts with the raid
## text: nine named turns with the pounding line each turn (`meanwhile`), and
## the expiry is the capture if she is anywhere but the closet — with teeth,
## §5's standing consequence; entering the closet stops it. The six rows are
## a five-turn clock's bodies and its expiry.
##
## GAPS (change document): the square's beats (two placeholders); how she
## leaves a square full of mercenaries (the source's blackout and FI22's
## "Shannon found you, after the crowd dispersed" are built as written);
## FI25's weight (carried as written, in the stub); the loose thread (nothing
## built).
##
## NOT CARRIED, with reasons:
##   * "without printing a room description" on the move to her bunk and the
##     source's `pause the game` — an authored move narrates its destination.
##   * The crowd's `[if the location is Lords Market]` tails — examine and
##     talk answer by room through refusals.
##   * `kiss fossville`, the source's `Chapter 7 - Mercenaries Again`
##     heading, the gallows as a supporter (Bobby and the Baron stand in the
##     square; "You can't reach him" is Bobby's talk refusal).
##   * The closet door "invisible before it has ever been opened" — the gap
##     is offstage until Shannon opens it, which is the same thing.
##   * `hide in the gap` rides the platform's hiding on the gap; bare `hide`
##     is a story action; both go in only from the Laundry with the gap open.
##   * Fiona's and Shannon's presence lines are now room markers (a presence
##     line cannot vary, GH #364): the kitchen's "washing the last of the
##     dinner dishes" and the laundry's "bent over the big washtub" print only
##     before the hanging, where the source's initial appearances stood.
## ===========================================================================

## ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
## THE HANGING (`story.ni:9585-9690`) — the state the streets read, and the
## clocks. `pending` until the butler is done with her; `gathering` while the
## crowd flows east; `speaking` once the Baron is on the platform; `over`
## after the blackout.
## ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

define timer execution for the player
end timer

define timer speech for the hanging
end timer

define timer verdict for the hanging
end timer

create the hanging
  aka bobby's hanging, the execution
  proper
  states: pending, gathering, speaking, over

  Bobby's hanging.

  when the player's execution expires
    move Bobby here
    move Baron Fossville here
    change the hanging to speaking
    phrase execution-b-h1
    start speech
  end when

  when speech expires
    move Hester Rudup here
    phrase execution-b-h2
    start verdict
  end when

  when verdict expires
    phrase execution-b-h3
    move Bobby offstage
    move Baron Fossville offstage
    move Hester Rudup offstage
    move the crowd of spectators offstage
    move the gallows offstage
    move the servants to the Lord's Market
    move Widow Fiona to the Dormitory
    move Widow Shannon to the Laundry
    change the hanging to over
    change the raid to waking
    move the player to the Dormitory
    phrase bedside-waking
    phrase fiona-fi21-stub
  end when

## `B_H1-3` (`story.ni:9662-9669`), verbatim.

define phrase execution-b-h1
  A man steps up onto the wooden platform. By squeezing between two
  spectators and craning your neck, you can just make out that it is Baron
  Fossville addressing the crowd.

  'Citizens of Toresal,' he shouts, 'as Lord of this City, it is my duty to
  administer justice within its gates, and punish those who transgress
  against the Queen's Law. More often than not, this duty is a heavy burden.
  Though I may wish to be lenient, consideration for the sanctity of the law
  and the safety of the Queen's subjects must always outweigh my softer
  instincts. Today, this burden weighs particularly upon me, for today I must
  administer the most stringent punishment allowed by the law.'

  Soldiers prod a second man up onto the platform. The black bag covers his
  face, but you recognize him by the clothes he's wearing — it's Bobby!
end phrase

define phrase execution-b-h2
  'This man before you stands accused of plotting high treason against the
  city of Toresal,' shouts the Baron. 'The evidence against him has been
  weighed, and he has been found guilty by the Honorable Magistrate Hester
  Rudup.'

  Fossville gestures to a third man standing on his other side, a gaunt man
  wearing crimson robes — the man who captured you last night at the
  fountain.
end phrase

define phrase execution-b-h3
  'Despite the grief it causes me, the penalty for this most heinous crime is
  clear.' Fossville pauses; the crowd is hanging on his every word. 'By the
  power vested in me and in accordance with the Queen's Law... I hereby
  sentence this man to hang by the neck until dead.'

  The Baron turns to one of his soldiers at the platform's base and nods his
  head.

  Later, you cannot remember whether you really heard the clunk of the
  trapdoor falling open, the rope snapping taut, or whether you only imagined
  it. You remember screaming, clawing at the spectators in front of you, and
  being pushed back by rough, angry hands. You stumbled and fell onto the
  cobblestones. You never saw his body drop.

  Everything goes black.
end phrase

