## THE RAID (`story.ni:9698-10006`) — the state Maiden House reads, and its
## two clocks. `pending` until the blackout; `waking` at the bedside;
## `pounding` from the raid text; `hidden` in the closet; `aftermath` from the
## closet to the expulsion and the key; `keyed` once she has it; `escaped`
## through the window, which is Chapter VIII's row.
## ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

## The pounding (`story.ni:9819-9821`): nine turns, the line each turn, the
## capture on the expiry unless she is in the closet.

define timer banging for the player
  pound-one
  pound-two
  pound-three
  pound-four
  pound-five
  pound-six
  pound-seven
  pound-eight
  pound-nine
  meanwhile
    phrase pounding-continues
end timer

## The six rows heard through the wall (`story.ni:9968-9982`), one a turn
## once she is inside: five as this clock's turns, the sixth on its expiry.
## The clock is the player's: a timer owned by an offstage entity speaks no
## turn bodies (the raid's own `overheard` printed nothing — GH #372).
## The captain's "she"/"the girl" are "he"/"the boy" (the ruling: they still
## hunt the boy Jack); Theresa's words are the boy words; Shannon's ruse is
## carried as written, "Jacqueline" and all.

define timer overheard for the player
  row-one
    The pounding suddenly stops. Heavy footsteps tromp into the building,
    making the floorboards shudder beneath your feet.

    'Where is he?' growls a rough voice. It's hard to tell from two rooms
    away, but it sounds very similar to the mercenary captain you heard
    yesterday morning, at the Grubber's Market.
  row-two
    'Where is who, sir?' That must be Fiona.

    'Don't play games with me, woman,' snaps the first voice. 'I've had my
    fill of it. The old Duke isn't here to protect you anymore, and if one of
    my men were to be careless and injure someone, it would rest but lightly
    on the Baron's conscience. Now, we know he came here after the hanging.
    Where is he?'

    'I can tell you, good sir,' says a third voice.

    Bitter anger wells up in your throat, even as fear turns your stomach
    cold. That was Theresa!
  row-three
    'Widow Theresa-' Fiona begins, in a strangled voice, but Theresa
    interrupts her.

    'Oh, do spare us your indignation, Fiona,' she snaps. 'I've stood by for
    years while you protected and coddled that child, and I tell you I've had
    my fill of it. Now he has done something unconscionable — obviously, or
    else the good Baron would not be looking for him — and I for one will not
    stand in the way of the law merely so that you can continue to bestow
    favoritism on one ungrateful little brat.'

    There is a long silence. You can feel your cheeks burning. Theresa has
    always disliked you, but you never thought she would be so treacherous as
    to turn you over to the Baron's men.
  row-four
    Finally, Theresa speaks: 'If you look in the laundry room, you will find
    a hidden closet in the north wall. The child is hiding there.'

    'My thanks, madam,' grunts the mercenary captain. 'I'll make sure the
    Baron hears of your cooperation when we-'

    Suddenly Shannon is screaming, 'Jacqueline! Jacqueline, wait!' Shouts.
    Running footsteps. What is going on? You hear footsteps coming down the
    hall, towards the laundry room... closer, closer... but then they
    suddenly turn away from the laundry room, towards... the privy?

    'Jacqueline... oh, Goddesses.' That's Shannon's voice.

    'What is it?' yells the mercenary captain. 'Where did the boy go?'

    'She... she got away from me, Miss Fiona,' wails Shannon. 'I was trying
    to get her into the hiding place, just like you said, but she slipped
    past me and wriggled out the privy window. You know how fast she can get
    through that window, Miss Fiona. She could be anywhere in the city by
    now. I'm so sorry, Miss Fiona, so sorry.'

    You can't help but grin. You've always thought of Shannon as being a
    trifle dim-witted, and now you'll have to revise your opinion.
  row-five
    The mercenary captain utters a curse so foul that you actually wince.
    'Don't just stand there, you bleeding dims,' he yells, 'get out there and
    find her! You two, keep an eye on the alley in case she comes back. Go!
    Go! Find her or I'll have your hides!' The footsteps run back up the
    hall, growing fainter this time, then vanishing altogether as they cross
    the threshold and exit Maiden House.

    You let out a long, shaky breath.
end timer

create the raid
  aka raid on maiden house, the baron's men
  proper
  states: pending, waking, pounding, hidden, aftermath, keyed, escaped

  The raid on Maiden House.

  when the player's banging expires, while the player is not in the Secret Closet
    kill the player raid-capture
  end when

  when the player's overheard expires
    phrase closet-door-opens
    change the raid to aftermath
    move the player to the Laundry
  end when

## The capture (`story.ni:9842`): "There he is!" (Theresa's boy words); the
## captain's "boy" as written.

define phrase raid-capture
  Suddenly the knocking pauses. Everyone seems to hold their breath for one
  silent moment.

  Then the front door crashes open and the mercenaries rush in. Children
  scream. Fiona stands in their way, shouting something you cannot hear.
  Scarred arms shove her brutally aside. Theresa points at you, crowing,
  'There he is!' but the men don't even look in her direction.

  The mercenary captain looms over you. His lip pulls back from chipped,
  yellow teeth.

  'You're beginning to damage my credibility, boy,' he growls.

  And then his fist crashes into the side of your head, and everything goes
  black.
end phrase

define phrase pounding-continues, randomly
  The pounding continues, rattling the door in its frame.
or
  The pounding continues. 'Open this door, widow, before we break it down!'
or
  The pounding continues. 'Open up!'
or
  The pounding continues.
end phrase

## `story.ni:9702`, the waking — Bedside Consolation begins.

define phrase bedside-waking
  You are lying on something soft. Blankets, a thin mattress — you're back in
  Maiden House, lying in your own bunk. Fiona is sitting on the edge of the
  bed, watching you with a tender expression. Her eyes are red and raw,
  though, like she's been crying. For a moment, you feel strange,
  disconnected. You wonder what's wrong.

  And then it comes back to you. It's Bobby. Bobby is dead.
end phrase

